How to use this plan
Give AI Studio one phase per message, in order. One giant prompt makes a coding agent skip or half-finish steps.
1.  Back up. Download the app as a ZIP before you start and before each phase, so you can roll back.
2.  Send the Master brief first. It tells the agent what the app is and which rules hold for every phase.
3.  Send one phase prompt per message, Phase 0 to Phase 8. Copy the whole grey code block for that phase.
4.  Check before moving on. Phase 0 adds a Diagnostics tab that simulates several days with a fake LLM in seconds. Run it after every phase and compare the numbers with the Target metrics section. If you run the code on your own machine, also run npm test.
5.  When a check fails, reply: "Stop. Re-read the Phase N prompt and the Master brief. List the acceptance checks that fail, then fix only those."
6.  Keep the order. Later phases depend on earlier ones; for example, Phase 5's meeting agreements need Phase 3's dated schedules. One exception: if the app is shared publicly, do Phase 2 (server security and cost caps) straight after Phase 0.
Phases 0 to 3 are the core and give the biggest gains. Phases 4 to 7 make the simulation behave like the Stanford paper. Phase 8 is polish.
Executive summary
The app is a polished simulator wrapped around a thinking loop that barely runs. At normal speed the town gets about 5 LLM conversations per simulated day; at 2x or 5x it gets none.
It is a Google AI Studio app (React and Vite in the browser, an Express server.ts) that recreates Stanford's Smallville with 18 residents on an 80×46 tile map. A pure TypeScript engine moves residents and tracks their needs, mood and memories. An LLM writes dialogue, reflections, daily plans and overnight memory summaries. The default is OpenRouter's free Nemotron model; Gemini is used if no OpenRouter key is set.
The biggest problems, all measured on the project's own code:
• One LLM job at a time. The app starts at most one job every 14 seconds and runs reflections before conversations. About 120 pairs a day qualify to talk; 5 conversations happen. 58% of LLM input tokens go to reflections.
• Memory is mostly noise. Each resident logs about 101 "who is in the room" notes a day. With the LLM off, they fill 92% of memory. The 65-item cap then deletes important memories; Isabella's party plan (importance 9) is gone by Day 3.
• The headline result is scripted. 17 of 18 residents have the Valentine's party written into their starting schedules. With no LLM at all, 16 of 18 attend every day.
• Time and sleep are broken. Schedules carry no date, so one-day detours repeat on later days and "tomorrow" meetings land on the wrong day. Nobody has a sleep block; by Day 4 at 3 AM, 7 of 18 residents are out of their homes.
• The server is open. Anyone with the app's address can overwrite the one shared save and spend your API key. The default free model allows 50 requests a day, under one simulated day of current traffic.
In this plan, "better" means numbers you can check in a new Diagnostics tab. Targets: at least 40 conversations per simulated day, every morning plan done before 8 AM, and noise under half of memory. Important memories must survive 7 days, and party attendance must come only from invitations. The plan also reports diffusion, attendance and network density next to the Stanford paper's results.
