What is good and must be preserved
Keep the engine design, the private per-speaker dialogue prompts and the JSON handling; every phase builds on them.
• A pure, fast engine. stepWorldState() takes a world and returns a new one with no side effects, at about 0.15 ms per tick. That is why every measurement in this doc could run headless, and why the Diagnostics tab is cheap to build.
• Private dialogue prompts. Each speaker is prompted separately with only its own memories plus what was said aloud. I checked the captured prompts: Maria's private plan text never appears in Isabella's prompt. Most Smallville clones use one all-knowing prompt for both sides.
• Robust LLM parsing. safeParseJson survives reasoning tags, code fences and extra prose; every number from the model is clamped; Gemini calls use a response schema.
• Clean world data. A validator over all 18 residents, 14 buildings and 32 objects found no broken references, no doors on walls and no objects reachable only from outside their building.
• Bounded storage. Caps on memories, chronicle and conversations keep a 7-day save near 614 KB.
• Good inspection tools. The inspector shows each memory's recency, importance and relevance scores. The interview, whisper and announce tools, Chronicle and Society views make the simulation explainable. The map canvas draws from a cached background at about 30 frames a second without going through React.
• A working test base. 12 node:test tests pass, and tsc reports no errors.
