Master brief (paste first)
Send this as your first message in AI Studio. It gives the agent the context and the rules every phase depends on.
You are improving "Smallville — Generative Agents", the app in this AI Studio project. It simulates 18 LLM-driven residents on an 80x46 tile map, modelled on Park et al. 2023, "Generative Agents: Interactive Simulacra of Human Behavior" (UIST '23).

I will send the work as Phases 0 to 8. Do only the phase I send, then stop and report.

How the app works now (keep this structure):
- src/simulation/ is a pure engine. engine.ts: stepWorldState() advances the world each tick; applyConversationResult(), applyReflectionResult() and applyConsolidationResult() merge LLM results. memory.ts: retrieval scoring and memory caps. psychology.ts: drives, mood and the System-1/System-2 encounter gate. pathfinding.ts: A*. seedAgents.ts and mapData.ts: residents, schedules, rumours ("memes"), buildings and objects. llmJson.ts: safeParseJson() and normalizers for model output. simulation.test.ts: node:test tests.
- src/App.tsx runs a 100 ms tick loop, decides which LLM job to start, and calls src/services/smallvilleApi.ts, which POSTs to server.ts (/api/agent/converse, /reflect, /plan, /interview, /whisper, /consolidate, /embed, and /api/world for saves).
- server.ts calls OpenRouter when OPENROUTER_API_KEY is set, otherwise Gemini (GEMINI_API_KEY, which AI Studio injects). Embeddings use gemini-embedding-2.

What an audit measured (each phase gives details): only one LLM job runs at a time and reflections go first, so a sim day gets about 5 conversations; memory is mostly "who is in the room" notes, and a 65-item cap deletes important memories; schedules have no dates and nobody sleeps; the Valentine's party is written into 17 residents' starting schedules; the API has no auth and one shared save file.

Rules for every phase:
1. Keep src/simulation/ pure: no React, no fetch, no Date.now(), no Math.random(). Pass time, IDs and random seeds in as arguments. New simulation logic goes there, not into components.
2. Keep API keys on the server. Never call LLM or embedding APIs from the browser.
3. Keep what works: the map canvas, Agent Inspector, Chronicle, Society and Architecture views, interview/whisper/announce, the separate per-speaker conversation prompts, safeParseJson and the llmJson normalizers.
4. Don't remove lines marked "Do not remove" in vite.config.ts.
5. Every behaviour change gets a node:test unit test in src/simulation/*.test.ts (or src/server/*.test.ts for server code). From Phase 0 on, the Diagnostics headless run must keep working.
6. After each phase, run npm run lint, npm test and npm run build, and fix what fails. Change an existing test only when the phase replaces the behaviour it checks, and tell me which.
7. When the saved-world shape changes, bump WORLD_SCHEMA_VERSION and add a migration. An old save must never crash the app: fall back to a fresh town and show a notice.
