Phase 1: A fair, parallel job scheduler
This is the biggest single gain. It replaces the one-job-every-14-seconds picker with a queue that runs several jobs at once, shares calls fairly, backs off on failures, and slows the clock when residents are waiting on the model.
Phase 1 — Cognition scheduler v2. Follow the Master brief rules.

Problem (measured with Diagnostics, 1x, 4 s per LLM call): about 120 resident pairs a day qualify to talk, but only about 5 conversations happen. One job runs at a time, a new one starts at most every 14 s, and reflections always go first (58% of input tokens go to reflections). At 2x and 5x there are 0 conversations in 4 days, and at 5x only half the residents get a morning plan. If one resident's plan call keeps failing, nobody else gets a morning plan (0 of 18 on days 2 to 4) and the call is retried about 49 times a day.

Build src/simulation/cognitionScheduler.ts (pure) and make App.tsx and headless.ts use it instead of cognitionPolicy.ts.

1. Jobs: { id, kind: 'morning_plan' | 'conversation' | 'reflection' | 'consolidation' | 'replan' | 'interview' | 'whisper', agentIds, expectedCalls, enqueuedAtMs, enqueuedAtSim, attempts }.
2. Concurrency: up to config.maxConcurrent jobs in flight (default 3). A resident can be in at most one in-flight job. Remove App.tsx's global single-flight lock; "busy" now means one or more jobs in flight.
3. Rate and budget: never exceed config.maxCallsPerMinute (default 20, counting each job's expected calls). Optional config.maxCallsPerSimDay. Fairness: subtract 10 priority points for each LLM call a resident has already used today, with a hard cap of 30 calls per resident per sim day. Presets: Thrifty (about 150 calls per sim day), Balanced (about 350), Research (about 600).
4. Priority with aging (higher runs first):
   - morning_plan 100: from 05:00 sim, for each resident not yet planned today.
   - user actions 95 (interview, whisper, Re-plan, Reflect Now, Sleep Cycle): never dropped silently; show "queued".
   - consolidation 70: once per resident per night, for ALL residents, not only the one selected in the UI. Use the window 22:30 to 03:00 until Phase 3 adds bedtimes.
   - conversation 60: from the engine's triggers. When the job starts, re-check that both residents are still within talking distance and free; drop stale pairs.
   - reflection 40: only if importanceAccumulator >= threshold, fewer than 3 reflections today, and at least 120 sim minutes since the resident's last one.
   - Aging: +1 point per sim minute a job has waited.
5. Failures: back off per (kind, resident). Retry after 15 s, 30 s, 60 s and so on, up to 5 min of real time, while other jobs keep running (no head-of-line blocking). After 3 failed morning plans, apply a fallback day (the resident's routine blocks moved to today) and mark the resident planned. After 3 failed reflections, halve the resident's accumulator. List failures in the UI, not only in the red banner.
6. Time dilation (config.timeDilation, on by default): count the due work — morning plans for residents whose first block starts within 60 sim minutes, conversations in progress, and queued conversations whose pair is still in range. When free lanes can't cover it, slow the sim clock by rate = clamp(freeLanes / dueJobs, 0.1, 1). Show "Slowed for thinking x0.4" in the header.
7. Conversations take sim time: when a conversation job starts, set inConversationWith and conversationStartedAtSim on both residents; they stop walking and face each other. Stamp the result at conversationStartedAtSim + turns x 1.5 sim minutes, not at the moment the HTTP reply arrives.
8. Rate-limit replies (429 or quota errors): pause only that job type, show a countdown, and resume automatically. Don't switch auto-cognition off globally.
9. Settings (in the Architecture view or a new Cognition panel): preset, maxConcurrent, calls per minute, time dilation on or off.

Tests (cognitionScheduler.test.ts): no resident in two jobs; priorities and aging; backoff timing; fallback plan after 3 failures; calls per minute respected; stale conversation pairs dropped; consolidation doesn't depend on the selected resident.

Acceptance (Diagnostics, fake LLM at 4 s per call, Balanced):
- 1x, 4 days: at least 40 conversations per sim day; all 18 morning plans done before 08:00 sim every day; 1 to 3 reflections per resident per day; consolidation for at least 16 of 18 residents every night.
- 5x with time dilation on: at least 20 conversations per sim day; all morning plans before 08:00.
- "First resident fails": the other 17 get plans every day; the failing one gets a fallback after 3 attempts and causes at most 3 failed calls a day.
- Never more than maxConcurrent jobs in flight, and never more than maxCallsPerMinute calls in any minute.
Phase 2: Server safety, cost limits and per-user saves
This closes the open API, caps spending, gives each browser its own town, and makes model calls fail safely. If the app is shared publicly, run this phase straight after Phase 0.
Phase 2 — Server safety, cost limits and per-user saves. Follow the Master brief rules.

Problem: every /api route is open. Anyone with the app's address can read, overwrite or delete the single shared save (.smallville-world.json) and spend your LLM key; when an AI Studio app is shared or deployed to Cloud Run, all users' calls run on your key. Every visitor shares one save file, so tabs overwrite each other and a visitor can load someone else's town; AI Studio's docs also say built-in storage isn't supported yet. The default model is an OpenRouter ':free' model, limited to 20 requests a minute and 50 a day (1,000 a day once you have bought at least 10 credits). Today's traffic is about 77 calls per sim day, and Phase 1 raises it. A reply that isn't valid JSON fails the whole conversation with no retry. The browser gives up at 75 s while the server can keep calling the model for about 5 minutes (3 calls in a row, each up to 3 attempts of 32 s).

1. Provider settings: env LLM_PROVIDER = auto | gemini | openrouter. 'auto' keeps today's rule (OpenRouter if OPENROUTER_API_KEY is set, else Gemini). GEMINI_MODEL env (default 'gemini-4-hydrogen'); optional per-job overrides MODEL_CONVERSE, MODEL_REFLECT and MODEL_PLAN. /api/health reports provider, models, limits and today's usage. If the OpenRouter model ends in ':free', show a warning with its daily limit in the UI.
2. Per-user worlds: the browser creates a worldId (crypto.randomUUID()), keeps it in localStorage and sends it as header x-world-id. Server saves are off by default (ENABLE_SERVER_SAVES=false). When on, store data/worlds/<uuid>.json, check the UUID format (no path traversal), and only ever serve a world to the worldId that wrote it. Remove the startup step that replaces the browser's town with a newer server save.
3. Browser saves: move the world snapshot from localStorage to IndexedDB (keep localStorage as a fallback). Add "Export world" (JSON download) and "Import world" buttons. Embeddings stay out of the snapshot.
4. Abuse and cost limits, with no new dependencies:
   - Token-bucket rate limiter per IP and per worldId on /api/agent/*: default 30 requests a minute, burst 10. Reply 429 with Retry-After.
   - Daily cap MAX_LLM_CALLS_PER_DAY (default 2000), counted per upstream call including retries. Reply 429 "daily budget reached"; the client pauses auto-cognition and says why.
   - Optional APP_ACCESS_TOKEN: when set, every /api request must send x-app-token. The UI asks for it once and stores it.
   - Body limits: 300 KB for /api/agent/*, 5 MB for /api/world. Validate each body by hand (types, arrays of at most 40 items, strings of at most 600 characters, IDs matching expected patterns) and reply 400 on failure. Put validators in src/server/validate.ts.
5. Reliability:
   - Per upstream call: a 25 s timeout and at most 1 retry for timeouts, 5xx or 429 (honour Retry-After). If the reply isn't valid JSON, make one repair call that adds: "Your last reply was not valid JSON. Reply with only the JSON object."
   - Each route gets a total deadline (converse 60 s, others 40 s). The browser timeout is the deadline + 10 s.
   - Abort upstream calls when the browser disconnects (req.on('close') -> AbortController).
   - For OpenRouter reasoning models, limit or exclude reasoning with OpenRouter's reasoning request option, so long reasoning can't use up max_tokens and cut off the JSON.
6. Metrics: /api/metrics returns, per job type: calls, failures, p50 and p95 latency, and input and output tokens (from the provider's usage fields). Log one structured line per upstream call.
7. README: costs and limits, the free-model daily cap, the fact that OpenRouter's free NVIDIA endpoint logs prompts, and how to set these env vars in AI Studio's Secrets panel.

Tests (src/server/*.test.ts, with a fake fetch): validators reject bad bodies; the rate limiter's bucket math; a non-JSON reply triggers exactly one repair call; the daily cap returns 429; worldId isolation.

Acceptance:
- POST or DELETE /api/world without x-world-id returns 400. With server saves off, they do nothing. Two worldIds never see each other's data.
- The 31st /api/agent request within a minute from one IP returns 429. With MAX_LLM_CALLS_PER_DAY=5, the 6th call returns 429 and the UI shows "daily budget reached" without crashing.
- Invalid bodies return 400. A non-JSON model reply gets one repair attempt, then a clear 502.
- In AI Studio with no OpenRouter key, /api/health shows provider "gemini".
Phase 3: Dated schedules, sleep and a believable world
This gives every plan a date, puts residents to bed, keeps agreed meetings through re-plans, and replaces word-matching shortcuts with explicit activity types.
Phase 3 — Dated schedules, sleep and a more believable world. Follow the Master brief rules.

Problem (measured, LLM off): schedule blocks are minutes-of-day with no date, so they repeat every day. One-day hunger and rest detours fired again on later days 8 times in 3 days. A commitment made at 15:00 for "10:00 tomorrow" is stored as today's minute 600. A mid-day re-plan keeps only past blocks, so future commitments vanish, and a morning plan replaces the whole day. No resident has a sleep block: after midnight they walk to their 08:00 place, so at 03:00 only 15, 13 and 11 of 18 are home on days 2 to 4 (others are in the park, at Town Hall or at Hobbs Cafe). Energy refills between 22:00 and 06:00 wherever they stand. Resting is detected by the substrings 'rest', 'break' and 'relax', so "forest", "interest" and "breakfast" count as rest. While residents walk outdoors (5% of the time), currentLocationId still names the last building.

1. Types: ScheduleBlock gets day: number; kind: 'sleep' | 'meal' | 'work' | 'social' | 'errand' | 'leisure' | 'rest' | 'travel'; fixed?: boolean (agreed meetings); and oneOff?: boolean (single events).
2. getActiveScheduleBlock(schedule, day, minuteOfDay) looks only at the current day's blocks. Before the first block: a synthetic "Sleeping" block at home (kind sleep) until wake time. After the last block ends: walk home, then sleep.
3. Day rollover at 00:00: drop past days' blocks. If a resident has no plan for the new day yet, copy its routine (seed blocks that aren't oneOff) into the new day, so nobody idles while waiting for a morning plan.
4. Seeds: add wake and sleep times for every resident (e.g. sleep 22:30 to 06:30 at home), give every seed block a kind, and mark one-time Day 1 events (the Valentine's party set-up and attendance) oneOff. 11 of 18 residents live in a public building (the college, cafe, pub, clinic, library, workshop, bakery or Town Hall). Keep that, but add a bed object inside each of those buildings and sleep there; the college bed area is the students' dorm. Update the object-count test.
5. Drives follow kinds, not words: energy refills only during sleep or rest blocks while the resident is at that block's place. Hunger refills only during meal blocks at a food place (cafe, pub, bakery or home). Drive detours are oneOff blocks for today, removed when finished. No hunger detours while asleep; schedule breakfast instead.
6. Commitments: store an absolute { day, minuteOfDay }. Use one shared constant for the allowed window (e.g. 06:00 to 22:30) in both llmJson.normalizeCommitment and the engine; today the parser allows 23:30 but the engine silently drops anything after 22:30. Reject times already past; allow tomorrow. Re-plans and morning plans keep every future fixed block, and the engine re-inserts any that a model reply leaves out.
7. Location: currentLocationId becomes string | null (null outdoors), plus currentAreaLabel for the UI (e.g. "Main Street"). Room perception and the "Here now" list use the real zone.
8. Talking range: a pair may talk only if both are in the same zone, or both are outdoors with no wall tile on the straight line between them (Bresenham over tiles), within 2.4 tiles.
9. Rumour keywords: match whole words or whole phrases after tokenizing, not substrings (today "run" in "brunch" spreads the mayor rumour).

Tests: a detour never fires on a later day; a commitment for tomorrow fires only tomorrow; a past commitment is rejected; a replan keeps fixed blocks; "Walking the forest trail" doesn't refill energy and sleeping does; walking outdoors sets currentLocationId to null; no conversation across a wall; an old (schema v1) save migrates.

Acceptance (Diagnostics, LLM off, 4 days): at least 17 of 18 residents at home at 03:00 every night; 0 detours repeating on a later day; 0 conversation pairs across a wall or between a building and the street; validateWorld reports nothing; the Day 1 party blocks don't appear on Day 2.
Phase 4: Memory that keeps what matters
This cuts room-presence noise, rates importance on a real 1–10 scale, forgets safely, and scores every memory's relevance on the same scale.
Phase 4 — Memory v2: less noise, real importance, safe forgetting, one retrieval scale. Follow the Master brief rules.

Problem (measured): each resident logs about 101 "who is in the room" notes per sim day. With the LLM off they make up 92% of stored memories; with it on, the 65 slots end up roughly half these notes and half reflections about them. Observation importance is a novelty score fixed to 4 to 7: "A fire broke out in the bakery and Mateo was hurt" and "Walked past a bench" both score 7. The 65-item cap fills within a day and then drops the oldest protected memories: Isabella's importance-9 party plan is gone by Day 3, and after 7 days only 73 Day-1 memories remain across all 18 residents. lastAccessed is never updated, so recency decays from creation, while the paper decays from last retrieval. With partial embedding coverage, (cos+1)/2 maps "unrelated" to 0.5, so an unrelated embedded memory gets relevance 0.40 and beats a relevant memory without an embedding (0.22). Background embedding (10 texts every 22 s) can't keep up with about 1,800 new memories a day.

1. Perception (engine.ts): create one observation when a resident enters a zone ("At Hobbs Cafe: Maria serving lattes, John reading"). While the resident stays, update that same memory (newcomers, notable activity changes, a new object trace) instead of adding new ones, and close it on leaving. Add a separate memory only for salient events: someone the resident has a social edge with arrives, a broadcast, an object changed by someone else, a conversation. Target: at most 30 observation memories per resident per sim day.
2. Importance on a 1 to 10 scale (memory.ts scoreImportance): base by kind (arrival 2, routine perception 2, meeting a known person 4, broadcast 8, plan 5 to 7, conversation = that participant's own model rating), plus 0 to 2 for novelty, plus 3 for salient words (fire, hurt, injured, accident, hospital, proposal, fight, award, election, invitation, party, wedding, death...), capped at 10. Optional "Paper mode": the model scores new memories in batches of 20 per resident, through the Phase 1 scheduler and budget.
3. Retention (replace compactMemories): configurable cap per resident, default 300. retainScore = importance/10 + 0.5 x recency + type bonus (reflection 0.3, consolidated 0.5, plan tied to a future fixed block 1.0) + 0.1 x times cited. Never evict a memory with importance >= 8 that is less than 7 sim days old. Before evicting routine observations older than 24 sim hours, compress each resident-day of them into one 'consolidated' summary memory that cites their IDs (rule-based, no LLM), so nothing disappears without a trace.
4. Access: when memories go into an LLM prompt (conversation, plan, reflection, interview, whisper), set their lastAccessed to the current sim time with markAccessed(world, agentId, ids, simMinutes). The Inspector's probe stays read-only.
5. One relevance scale: if at least 90% of the candidate memories and the query have embeddings of the same size, relevance = 0.8 x min-max-scaled cosine + 0.2 x lexical. Otherwise use lexical for all candidates. Remove the (cos+1)/2 mapping.
6. Embeddings (only when GEMINI_API_KEY is set): embed every new memory when it is written, through a client queue (batches of up to 32, at most one request every 3 s, counted in the Phase 1 budget). Google recommends task prefixes for text-only gemini-embedding-2: send queries as "task: search result | query: {text}" and memories as "title: none | text: {text}". Cache vectors in IndexedDB keyed by the SHA-256 of model + dimensions + prefixed text (crypto.subtle), not by memory ID.
7. Queries: build each resident's retrieval query from its own situation. Remove the hard-coded "party mayor town" words in smallvilleApi.ts.
8. Consolidation may prune only existing memories with importance <= 4; ignore any other IDs the model returns.

Tests: one perception memory per visit unless something salient happens; importance examples (fire >= 8, bench <= 3); retention never drops importance >= 8 within 7 days and writes summaries; markAccessed raises recency; mixed coverage falls back to lexical; consolidation can't prune an importance-9 memory. Update the existing compactMemories and consolidation tests.

Acceptance (Diagnostics, fake LLM, 7 days): perception notes at most 50% of stored memories; at most 30 observation memories per resident per day; every seed memory with importance >= 8 present or cited by a summary; 0 duplicate IDs; memories per resident within the cap; saved world under 5 MB.
Phase 5: Conversations with natural length and shared agreements
This lets talks run 2 to 6 turns, stops one resident's private plans from steering the other's memory, and turns spoken agreements into the same meeting in both calendars.
Phase 5 — Conversations v2: natural length, no leaks, shared agreements. Follow the Master brief rules.

Problem (measured through the real server with a stubbed model): every conversation is exactly 3 calls (A, B, A), about 4,000 input tokens. B writes its memory of the talk before hearing A's last line, and A's importance rating is stored for both. Each resident's memory query includes the partner's private intention: changing only Maria's private plan changed 2 of the 8 memories Isabella recalled (the text itself never reaches Isabella's prompt, which is good). Agreements are one-sided: Maria agreed to meet, but no block was added for Isabella. Rumour spread is decided by keyword substrings.

1. Turn loop (server.ts /api/agent/converse; keep the separate per-speaker prompts): 2 to MAX_TURNS spoken lines (default 6), alternating. Each turn is one call with only the speaker's private context and the spoken transcript so far, returning { spokenLine, privateThought, endConversation }. Stop when a speaker ends it (after at least 2 turns) or at MAX_TURNS.
2. Recall per turn: the client sends, for each resident, a candidate pool (top 30 by importance, recency and relevance to that resident's own situation; descriptions only). The server re-ranks that pool every turn with the shared retrieveMemories, using the speaker's own intention, the partner's name and visible action, and the last spoken line. Never use the partner's private 'currently' or memories. Call markAccessed (Phase 4) for the memories used.
3. After the dialogue, make two calls in parallel, one per participant, each with the full transcript and its own private context. Each returns { summary, importance (1 to 10), planUpdate?, emotionalShift, tomUpdate, memeIdsDiscussed (chosen from the rumours this resident knew or just heard; send their ids and topics), newNews: [{ topic, keywords }] }.
4. Agreements: from the spoken transcript, the initiator's wrap-up call also extracts at most one shared agreement { day: 'today' | 'tomorrow', minuteOfDay, locationId, activity }. Apply it to BOTH residents' schedules as the same fixed block with an absolute day (Phase 3 rules). Reject past times.
5. Starting a talk: keep the System-1/System-2 gate, and add a reason (has news the partner lacks, has a goal involving the partner, close rapport plus social need). Pass the reason to the initiator's first turn ("You want to tell Maria about...").
6. Sim time: a conversation lasts turns x 1.5 sim minutes from its start. Speech bubbles show each line in order during that window. ConversationRecord stores reason, turns, endedBy and agreement.
7. Rumours: spread only through memeIdsDiscussed, checked against the list sent. newNews creates new rumours with the speaker as origin; skip near-duplicates of existing rumours (token overlap, or embeddings when available).

Tests: resident A's prompts and recalled memories are identical whatever B's private intention is; the turn cap and endConversation work; each participant gets its own summary and importance; one agreement gives both residents the same fixed block; rumours spread only through validated ids. Update the existing conversation test.

Acceptance: Diagnostics (fake LLM) reports average turns and calls per conversation, never more than MAX_TURNS + 2 calls; the leak test passes; no agreement lands in the past.
Phase 6: Reflection, planning and reactions as in the paper
This rebuilds reflection as the paper's two-step process with checked evidence, plans whole days top-down, and adds the missing step where residents react to what they see.
Phase 6 — Reflection, planning and reactions as in the paper. Follow the Master brief rules.

Problem: reflection is one prompt over 22 retrieved memories. Its "focal questions" never drive retrieval, and its evidence IDs are never checked (a made-up ID was stored as-is). Residents never react to what they see; only conversations and whispers change plans. In Park et al. 2023, residents reflect when the summed importance of recent events passes a threshold (150 in their implementation), about two or three times a day. They start from the 100 most recent memories and 3 questions. They plan top-down: a day plan of 5 to 8 chunks, then hourly, then 5 to 15 minute steps. At each step they decide whether to react; if they do, the plan is regenerated from that moment.

1. Reflection, 2 calls: call 1 gets the 100 most recent memories (or those since the last reflection, at least 20) and returns 3 focal questions. The client retrieves the top 12 memories per question with retrieveMemories. Call 2 gets the three question-and-evidence sets and returns up to 5 insights, each citing evidence IDs from those sets. Drop cited IDs that weren't provided, and drop insights left with no valid evidence. Reflections may cite earlier reflections. Calibrate REFLECTION_THRESHOLD to the Phase 4 importance scale so Diagnostics shows 1.5 to 3 reflections per resident per day; never more than 3.
2. Morning plan (05:00 to 07:00 sim, through the scheduler). Inputs: identity, yesterday's consolidated lessons, today's fixed commitments listed as constraints, and drives. Output: 5 to 8 blocks from wake-up to sleep, each with kind, place and optional object, including meals. The engine checks: no overlaps, inside the day, valid place and object, and every fixed block kept (re-inserted if missing).
3. Step detail on demand: when a block starts without subSteps, ask for 3 to 5 sub-steps with minutes (a cheap call, only when the budget allows); otherwise split the block by rules.
4. Reaction step: when a resident gets an observation with importance >= 6, or meets someone it has news or a goal for, enqueue a 'reaction' job (1 call) returning { react: boolean, reason, newActivity?, talkTo? }. If react is true, replace the rest of the current block (fixed blocks stay) or start a conversation. At most 4 reactions per resident per day.
5. Re-plan (button and reactions) replaces only the blocks between now and the next fixed block.

Tests: made-up evidence IDs dropped; an insight with no valid evidence dropped; the plan checker keeps commitments and rejects overlaps; a broadcast (importance 8) creates a reaction job; reactions capped at 4 a day.

Acceptance (Diagnostics, fake LLM, 4 days): 1.5 to 3 reflections per resident per day; every morning plan has sleep and at least 2 meals; 0 commitments lost to re-plans; at most 4 reactions per resident per day.
Phase 7: Remove the script and measure emergence
This is where the app earns the Smallville name: only Isabella knows about the party at the start, and attendance comes from invitations alone. The app then measures spread, attendance and relationship growth against the paper.
Phase 7 — Emergence: remove the script and measure it like the paper. Follow the Master brief rules.

Problem: 17 of 18 residents have a Hobbs Cafe block between 16:00 and 17:30 in their starting schedules, so with the LLM off, 16 of 18 are at the party every day. Nothing has to spread for the party to happen. In Park et al. 2023, only Isabella knew about the party and only Sam knew about his candidacy at the start. Over two game days with 25 agents, the party news reached 13 agents (52%) and the candidacy 8 (32%); 12 were invited and 5 attended; network density rose from 0.167 to 0.74.

1. Scenario "Valentine's party (paper replication)": start Day 1 at 09:00. The party is on Day 2 from 17:00 to 19:00 at Hobbs Cafe, which gives the news two days to spread, as in the paper. Only Isabella knows (a memory plus a goal to invite neighbours); only Sam knows about his candidacy. Remove party blocks and party memories from every other resident and give them their normal routines. meme_valentine_party.knownBy = { isabella }; meme_mayor_race.knownBy = { sam }. Keep the other two rumours as they are.
2. Goals: add goals: string[] to AgentState (Isabella: "Invite as many neighbours as possible to my Valentine's party at Hobbs Cafe, Day 2 at 5 PM"). Goals feed the conversation reason, reactions and plans.
3. Attendance only through decisions: a resident goes to the party only through a fixed block from a conversation agreement, or a block in their own plan made after they heard the news.
4. Experiments tab: run the scenario live (real LLM, with the budget shown) or headless (fake LLM). Show awareness over time for each rumour; invited, agreed and attended (inside Hobbs Cafe for at least 20 minutes of the party); network density over time (edges = pairs who have talked; density = 2|E| / (|V| x (|V| - 1))); conversations; and LLM calls and tokens. Draw the paper's figures as reference lines. Export the run log as JSON.
5. Ablations (switches): no reflection; no planning (fixed routine); no memory retrieval (only the 10 most recent memories); no System-2 gate.
6. Interview battery: 25 questions per resident in the paper's five groups (self-knowledge, memory, plans, reactions, reflections). Store the answers; optionally score them 1 to 5 with an LLM judge, and compare ablations.

Tests: with the LLM off, only Isabella goes to the party; with a fake LLM that accepts half of all invitations, every attendee has an agreement block or a plan block made after hearing the news; density is correct on a toy graph.

Acceptance: Diagnostics with the LLM off shows party attendance of 1 (Isabella). A fake-LLM run shows attendance above 1 only through agreements. The Experiments tab shows awareness %, attendance and density next to the paper's 52% / 32%, 5 of 12, and 0.167 to 0.74.
Phase 8: UI speed, visibility into thinking, and polish
This stops the 10-per-second full re-render, shows what the model is doing and what it costs, and makes every button give feedback.
Phase 8 — UI speed, visibility into thinking, and polish. Follow the Master brief rules.

Problem: App.tsx calls setWorldSnapshot every 100 ms, so every panel re-renders 10 times a second. The Agent Inspector takes about 4 to 5 ms per render and re-runs memory retrieval each tick (about 2 ms with 300 memories), which grows with Phase 4's larger memory. "LLM Calls Saved" counts every quick greeting as a saved call (1,886 in a 7-day run against 38 real conversations). Some buttons silently do nothing while another job runs.

1. State: keep the world in an external store read with useSyncExternalStore and selectors. Panels update at most 4 times a second; the canvas keeps reading the ref every frame. The Inspector subscribes only to the selected resident; memoize SocietyView; virtualize long lists (memories, chronicle).
2. Cognition panel: jobs in flight; the queue with priorities and wait times; failures and backoff; calls per minute; calls and tokens today; estimated cost (you enter prices per million input and output tokens); the current time-dilation factor.
3. Honest stats: rename "LLM Calls Saved" to "Quick greetings (no LLM)", and add "LLM calls made" and "Tokens".
4. Memory inspector: show importance, lastAccessed and source (perception, conversation, reflection, broadcast, summary); make evidence in reflections and summaries clickable; search lexically or semantically.
5. Conversation viewer: full transcript, a toggle for private thoughts, the reason for talking, and any agreement.
6. Map: a line between residents who are talking, "Zzz" for sleepers, and a label for outdoor areas.
7. Feedback: every action shows queued, running or failed; no silent no-ops.

Tests: store selectors; a dev-only overlay that counts React commits per second.

Acceptance: at most 5 React commits per second while playing at 1x; smooth scrolling with 300 memories; every button gives visible feedback.
